WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:09.660
For a decade Washington, DC, has been a
celebrated national model of school

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:14.849
reform. Then, in January, and audit showed that one third of DC's high school

00:00:14.849 --> 00:00:18.690
graduates should not have received
diplomas. Indeed, it turns out that the

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:23.820
system's true graduation rate is lower
than it was in 2011. How could this have

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:28.380
gone unnoticed? Blogger Alexander
Rousseau has placed part of the blame on

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:32.790
rank boosterism by the capital's
preeminent news outlet. But this coverage

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:38.450
reflects a larger problem: the bandwagon
mentality in school reform. Since 2007,

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:42.030
philanthropists have poured more than a
hundred and twenty million dollars into

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:46.680
DC schools, and advocates and think tanks were lauded for glowing accounts of

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.149
developments in DC. It was easy for
reformers to feel they'd be labeled

00:00:51.149 --> 00:00:56.070
unhelpful or anti reform for going
off-script. This all fueled a "with us or

00:00:56.070 --> 00:01:00.149
against us" mindset in which no one
looked too hard for problems.

00:01:00.149 --> 00:01:04.049
DC should be a wake-up call for a
school reform culture that has been

00:01:04.049 --> 00:01:07.340
heavy on cheerleading and light on
skepticism.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:14.340
What do you make of the Washington, DC, graduation rate scandal? Let us know in your comments.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:20.939
Also, let us know what other topics you'd like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be

00:01:20.939 --> 00:01:27.650
sure to like and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

